  "nameStory": "The Straight-stalked Entoloma gets its name from its remarkably rigid, upright stem that stands as straight as a soldier at attention. Unlike many of its relatives that develop curved or twisted stems, this species maintains perfect posture throughout its life. The scientific name 'strictius' comes from Latin meaning 'more strict' or 'more upright,' emphasizing this defining characteristic.",
  "description": "The Straight-stalked Entoloma is a medium-sized mushroom with a distinctive no-nonsense appearance. It features a brownish to grayish-brown cap that's typically 1-3 inches across, sitting atop that famously straight stem. Like all members of the pinkgill family, its gills start white but develop a salmon-pink color as the spores mature. This species has a clean, understated look compared to some of its more colorful relatives.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Perfectly straight and rigid stem, 2-4 inches tall, that feels firm and fibrous when gently squeezed. The stem maintains its upright posture even in windy conditions.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Brown to grayish-brown cap, 1-3 inches wide, often with a slightly depressed center and smooth, silky surface that feels dry to the touch.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "White gills when young that gradually turn salmon-pink as spores develop. Gills are attached to the stem and moderately spaced.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Salmon-pink to rose-colored spore print, characteristic of all pinkgill mushrooms. Place cap gill-side down on white paper overnight to see this feature.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Mild, slightly earthy odor with no strong or unpleasant scents. Some describe a faint flour-like smell when crushed.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, particularly favoring areas with rich, moist soil and plenty of leaf litter. Often appears near oak, maple, and beech trees where it feeds on decomposing organic matter. Look for it along forest edges and in clearings where dappled sunlight reaches the forest floor.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Rarely fruits in spring, though occasional specimens may appear during warm, wet periods in late May.",
    "summer": "Peak season begins in mid to late summer, especially after periods of steady rain when soil moisture is high.",
    "fall": "Most abundant in early to mid-fall when temperatures cool but soil remains moist from autumn rains. Fruiting typically ends with first hard frost.",
    "winter": "Not active during winter months; mushrooms decompose and mycelium remains dormant in soil."
  },
  "funFact": "The Straight-stalked Entoloma's stem is so consistently upright that mycologists sometimes use it as a natural plumb line when photographing other mushrooms in the field to ensure their cameras are level!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most commonly confused with other brown pinkgills, but the perfectly straight stem is usually a dead giveaway. Remember: 'Straight name, straight stem' - if the stem has any curve or lean, you're probably looking at a different species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The pinkgill family (Entolomataceae) is notorious among mushroom hunters for being difficult to identify to species level, with many members requiring microscopic examination of spores. However, the Straight-stalked Entoloma is one of the few that can be confidently identified in the field by its distinctive posture.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike the Aborted Entoloma which often has deformed, blob-like fruiting bodies, or the colorful Salmon Pinkgill with its bright orange-pink tones, the Straight-stalked Entoloma maintains a subdued brown appearance and that signature rigid stem. While Wood Pinkgill grows directly on decaying logs, this species fruits from soil and leaf litter. The straight stem immediately separates it from nearly all other pinkgills in New England.",
